Now, this is the part that is really interesting to me. Because by the 
time I get a character to the Quest, I consider him *highly* ascendable, 
as in, "This character *will* win barring YASSSSSSD."

So what is it about a player who can get a character several levels deep 
in the dungeon, screw around long enough to dig out or rob a significant 
amount of money, and parlay that money into a significant AC boost, all 
at level one, and then *with this advantage,* blow the game beyond that 
point?

If the racket is such a huge boost when successfully completed, we 
should be seeing racketeers regularly ascending, far more often than 
other players. The benefits of the strategy should be unquestionable.

The fact that we don't see that suggests to me that playing the racket 
actually trains players in modes of play that are not actually tenable 
throughout the whole game, leading to more midgame and late game deaths.

